infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535041 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19c55ecaf73ac1c0c8db619e6c43d14f35e50e2321ccae5687b3a1bc6a687563.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535062 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d1470c8-ba27-52a6-a01f-3373f12de77d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535107 